Since $a_i$ never exceeds 210, there are at most 210 possible terms. But there is certainly no reason to expect any of these terms to exhaust the possible possibilities. What we want to do is build a specific sequence of numbers $a_i$ with remainder terms $b_i$ that manages to cover $0, 1, \ldots , 210$ as $b_i$. This is easy to do if $a_i$ increases geometrically. For example, let $a_i = 2^i$. Then it is clear that every term $b_i = 2^i \mod 210$ takes a different value. For example, \[ \begin{array}{l l c} b_1 & = 2 \mod 210 \\b_ 2 & = 4 \mod 210 \\b_3 & = 8 \mod 210 \\b_4 & = 16 \mod 210 \\b_5 & = 32 \mod 210 \\b_6 & = 64 \mod 210 \\b_7 & = 128 \mod 210 \\b_8 & = 29 \mod 210 \\b_9 & = 58 \mod 210 \\ b_ { 10 } & = 17 \mod 210 \\b_ { 11 } & = 34 \mod 210 \\b_ { 12 } & = 68 \mod 210 \\b_ { 13 } & = 136 \mod 210 \\b_ { 14 } & = 19 \mod 210 \\b_ { 15 } & = 38 \mod 210\\b_ { 16 } & = 76 \mod 210 \\b_ { 17 } & = 119 \mod 210 \\b_ { 18 } & = 59 \mod 210 \\b_ { 19 } & = 175 \mod 210 \\ b_ {20} & = 4 \mod 210 \\ b_ {21} & = 8\mod 210 \\ \ldots & \end{array} \]Notice when we cycle through, the remainders hit all the numbers coprime to $210 = 2 \times 3 \times 5 \times 7$, and also $0$, $15$, and $35$. These numbers are mutually relatively prime, since $\gcd(15, 35) = 5$ and $5 \nmid 2$. Therefore, by Dirichlet's theorem, there are infinitely many primes with residue $1 \mod 15$ and corresponding residue pairs $20 \mod 210$ and $135 \mod 210$, and infinitely many primes with residue $1 \mod 35$ and corresponding residue pairs $135 \mod 210$ and $90 \mod 210$. Therefore, there is some prime with residue $1 \mod 210$ and residue $1$. Thus, we have a sequence of $a_i$ with infinite distinct remainders, $0, b_1, b_2, \ldots$ with $b_i \mod 210 = 210 \times i \mod 210$, so there are at least $210 +1 = \boxed{211}$ terms.h7
